00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.610
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG:
Thanks, Alicia.

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:52.630
ALICIA PATERSON: So
today we're going

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.240
to spend a little bit of time
talking about what it means

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.790
to build API products,
and then Erin's

00:00:57.790 --> 00:00:59.440
going to talk
about how Walgreens

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:03.700
has been able to use APIs
to extend their assets

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.930
into developer ecosystems.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.170
And then that'll be followed by
responding to your questions,

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.120
so let's jump into it.

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.590
So what do we mean when we
talk about APIs as products,

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.070
and how can productizing
APIs provide opportunities

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.610
for business?

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:22.510
This can be a difficult
concept for product people

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.600
to get their head
around, as at best, APIs

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.920
are seen as points of technical
integration and as at worse,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.350
IT jargon.

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:34.360
I have a simple example
from the insurance business

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.820
that I find product
professionals can often

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:40.180
relate to, and it helps
illustrate the ability for APIs

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:43.420
to extend into new
channels of opportunity

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:47.510
while also extending a
better customer experience.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.820
So no one really enjoys
shopping for insurance.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:54.010
It's one of those
necessary things

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.540
that we have in our lives.

00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.720
Insurance companies have a tough
time creating a great customer

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.360
experience because the
times you typically

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.420
engage with an insurance company
is when you have to pay them

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.460
a bunch of money
to buy insurance

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.919
or when you make a claim
because something that you hoped

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.541
wouldn't happen just happened.

00:02:10.541 --> 00:02:12.790
So I was thinking about this
recently because I bought

00:02:12.790 --> 00:02:14.911
a drone, like an
unmanned aerial vehicle,

00:02:14.911 --> 00:02:16.660
and I was concerned
that it might fall out

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.660
of the sky from time to time
and land on something expensive.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.520
So I went shopping
for insurance,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.610
and I found this product offer
by a large, multinational

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.260
insurance company.

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:28.030
And they make the
product available

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.180
through the traditional high
commission agent network

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.690
as well as directly online.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:35.020
It was like $800 for a
million dollars of coverage

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:36.610
that lasted a year.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.746
I thought it was a bit
pricey for me, probably

00:02:38.746 --> 00:02:40.870
a bit pricey, too, for more
than the half a million

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.870
people that bought consumer
drones in the US last year.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.920
And then I found a mobile
app, more of a niche insurance

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:48.672
offering.

00:02:48.672 --> 00:02:50.380
When you launch this
app, it draws a map,

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.360
drops a pin where
you are, and says

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.060
if you want to fly your drone
for the next hour anywhere

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:58.180
in that radius, then
it costs like $20

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.770
or something for a million
dollars of coverage,

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.720
which was a much better
offering for a casual flyer.

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:06.280
And that made me think.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.620
The insurance
company's core business

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.050
is assessing an
underwriting risk

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:14.590
and putting a price on that,
and obviously having a margin

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:16.210
where they make their business.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:20.640
Had they been API first
and productized to the API,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.410
it would have allowed them
to extend their core business

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:25.420
to power this app,
the one that I found,

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:27.340
and indeed, many others like it.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:30.070
And what's more, if they
had APIs to quote and sell

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:32.080
their drone
insurance, they could

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:34.210
give it to drone
manufacturers who

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.630
then could build it directly
into their product experience.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:39.540
When you use the
app or whatever tool

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.860
you use to control your
drone, one of the options

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:44.056
might include purchasing
insurance for that flight.

00:03:44.056 --> 00:03:45.680
And surely there's
no better experience

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.670
than not having to worry about
going and getting insurance

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:51.340
before you can go
flying with your drone.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.830
Instead, you can just fly your
drone with the peace of mind

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:56.246
that you can purchase insurance
for that particular flight

00:03:56.246 --> 00:03:57.590
if you need to.

00:03:57.590 --> 00:04:00.640
And it also occurred to me that
the insurance company today

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.610
uses, obviously, a lot of data
to inform its risk assessment

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:04.619
and pricing.

00:04:04.619 --> 00:04:06.160
However, typically
the only data it's

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.140
able to collect when
running its business

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.890
is when someone buys a policy
or when they make a claim.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.830
Whereas if they were to
productize to the API

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:17.440
and serve applications
like the one that I found,

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.060
then they could
also have visibility

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.550
of how often people
fly their drones

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.230
and where they fly their
drones and for how long--

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.040
data that could significantly
enrich their ability

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.560
to assess that insurance risk.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.990
Productizing the API, creating
an API to quote and sell

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:34.840
drone insurance,
and then offering it

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.300
to an external ecosystem
of app developers and drone

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.690
manufacturers would not
only extend their product

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.730
to a much greater
reach, it would also

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:45.700
give them richer data with
which to make their underwriting

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:46.732
assessment.

00:04:46.732 --> 00:04:48.940
Not to mention, they could
also use those APIs, then,

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:53.830
to power their traditional
agent network and their website.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.430
So what does
productizing an API mean?

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.500
There are many aspects
that we might talk about,

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.270
but for this webcast,
we're just going

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:04.090
to focus on these three items.

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:08.950
So outside-in means
developing a strong focus

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.530
on the external
business or user need

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:13.340
and making sure
your organization

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:17.750
connects that perspective
directly to development work.

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:19.820
We'll talk more on
that in a moment.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:23.240
Speed to market recognizes the
cost of software and supporting

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.795
infrastructure is lower now
than it ever has been before,

00:05:26.795 --> 00:05:28.730
thanks to growth in
Cloud driving down

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:31.370
infrastructure costs,
software frameworks

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.220
with built-in foundations
that speed development,

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:38.030
and open source platforms allow
for maximum collaboration.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:40.460
Because of this, the manner
in which software is built

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:44.900
needs to change to modern agile
practices with a strong focus

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.020
on removing obstacles
to fast delivery.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:51.050
The digital products are
never really finished, right?

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:54.140
Because there's always some
new technology or new device

00:05:54.140 --> 00:05:58.010
that your customers expect
to engage you across.

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:02.270
Iterative delivery asks that
you take an iterative product

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.210
delivery approach to your APIs--

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:07.340
that you have a
product backlog, you

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.700
make regular ongoing
improvements to them over time,

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:13.820
the same as you would for your
website or your mobile apps

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.874
or indeed any other
product that you might own.

00:06:16.874 --> 00:06:18.290
So each of these
we can break down

00:06:18.290 --> 00:06:20.300
into further supporting ideas--

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:22.130
a couple of which
I'll talk about now--

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.960
but also much is covered
in our "Complete Guide

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.580
to API Products" eBook,
the link for which

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:32.070
we'll provide a little bit
later on in this webcast.

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:36.230
So lets look more
closely at outside-in.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.300
Outside-in is a mindset.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.490
It's a design
philosophy supported

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:43.730
by organizational constructs,
such as team and role

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:47.390
definitions and software
development methodologies.

00:06:47.390 --> 00:06:50.450
It moves a problem domain
from an IT question

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.320
of exposing data or
other assets instead

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:56.990
to the question of how that data
will be used from a consumption

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.400
perspective.

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.770
Traditionally, IT
teams think about APIs

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:02.630
from an exposure point of view--

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:05.750
we can just expose a
service and be finished.

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.450
This brings with it a risk
of surfacing complexity

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.580
since the people
who build solutions

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:13.910
on your backend systems
are close to their systems

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:16.670
and [INAUDIBLE] in
their idiosyncrasies.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:19.000
If your API development
team consists of just

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:20.780
your backend
developers, then you

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:23.940
run the risk of exposing your
complexity with your data,

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:27.920
whether it be complicated call
outs to disparate services

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.170
or an assumed understanding
of the underlying data

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:31.790
architecture.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:35.330
Exposing complexity creates
friction for the developers

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:38.660
who will consume your APIs,
slowing down their adoption

00:07:38.660 --> 00:07:40.340
and their ability
to build solutions

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.780
on top of your assets.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.460
However, thinking about it from
the end user's point of view

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:48.950
leads to better results
for the business.

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:52.760
Apigee's most successful
customers start from the user,

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:55.400
thinking about what the user's
experience will be like,

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.470
and working
backwards from there.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:01.070
Providing the application
developer with great APIs

00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:05.150
reduces the complexity they face
in creating new applications.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.700
The application developer
is free to focus

00:08:07.700 --> 00:08:12.020
on user experience and iterate
on a few different designs.

00:08:12.020 --> 00:08:14.840
The API team makes this
possible by providing

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:19.580
easy to use APIs with consistent
interfaces and security

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:20.930
patterns.

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:23.570
The API team builds
an API facade

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:26.840
on the existing services
based on an understanding

00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:29.480
of the application
developer's needs

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:32.270
and abstracting away
the complexities.

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:34.820
One way to ensure that you
bring an outside-in perspective

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:37.700
to development is to make
sure that your development

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:41.419
team includes a product
owner, ideally a product

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:44.990
manager for the API,
or the relevant product

00:08:44.990 --> 00:08:47.240
owner for that
business area, someone

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:49.280
who is close to the
needs of the users

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:52.280
and the app developers that
are creating software for them.

00:08:55.250 --> 00:08:57.810
So packaging up an
API as a product

00:08:57.810 --> 00:08:59.900
means considering
the outside-in design

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:02.730
and making it easy for
those consuming developers

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:05.730
to find and use your APIs
to solve the software

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:07.800
problems that they
face, helping them

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.990
with things like
great documentation

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:15.360
that makes it clear what the API
does and how it might be used.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.170
Like with e-commerce, your
goal is to make the experience

00:09:19.170 --> 00:09:21.100
as frictionless as possible--

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.340
remove barriers to
discovery and adoption

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:25.530
by your app developers.

00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:27.780
In the same way an
online retailer seeks

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:31.440
to remove unnecessary clicks
between a customer's purchase

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:35.520
decision and their check out,
in the API product world,

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:36.960
you should be
looking to minimize

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.330
the time it takes for
your app developers

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:42.630
to get to their first hello
world, their first use

00:09:42.630 --> 00:09:45.060
of your APIs.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.000
So what does an API product
experience look like?

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:52.470
I have a conceptual model
that presents a metaphor,

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:55.260
if you like, for the
API product experience

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:57.669
from an app developer's
perspective.

00:09:57.669 --> 00:09:59.460
So for this metaphor,
I want you to imagine

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:02.050
that your 8-year-old
niece, it's her birthday,

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:04.486
and she's asked you
for a LEGO castle.

00:10:04.486 --> 00:10:05.860
So you go down to
the LEGO store.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.700
When you walk in, you see
rows of LEGO boxes on shelves.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:12.280
So the whole catalog
is on display,

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:14.220
making it really easy
for you to quickly find

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:16.056
the box that has a
picture of a castle on it

00:10:16.056 --> 00:10:17.430
that looks like
the kind of thing

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:18.930
you think she might enjoy.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.650
When you look at the box, you
see that it says ages 6 to 65.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:26.200
It describes the target
audience for the product.

00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:28.200
It has pictures on the
box of what the completed

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.049
set will look like.

00:10:29.049 --> 00:10:30.840
In fact, there are
pictures of other things

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.530
you can use the
contents to build--

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:35.992
they're like other use cases
for the contents of the box.

00:10:35.992 --> 00:10:37.700
You're pretty sure
it's what she'll want,

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:39.616
but you're even more
sure when you see a glass

00:10:39.616 --> 00:10:43.115
case on display nearby where
one has been assembled already.

00:10:43.115 --> 00:10:44.580
And you can look
at that one, it's

00:10:44.580 --> 00:10:47.130
like a reference
implementation for the product.

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.769
So confident it's exactly what
she'll love, you can buy it.

00:10:49.769 --> 00:10:51.560
You're out of the store
within five minutes

00:10:51.560 --> 00:10:54.210
knowing that you've solved
the problem that you have.

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:56.370
Now imagine that experience
built by someone close

00:10:56.370 --> 00:11:00.060
to the backend that might
take and exposure approach,

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.620
like an inside out approach.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.797
In that case, when you go
down to the LEGO store,

00:11:03.797 --> 00:11:05.380
you can't walk
straight in, of course,

00:11:05.380 --> 00:11:08.110
because you need to first have
an NDA and contract signed

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:09.580
to let you come into the store.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:11.550
And then once you can
come into the store,

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:14.430
rather than seeing rows
of products on shelves,

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:18.420
you see buckets and buckets of
all different types of LEGOs

00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:20.300
in all different types of color.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:22.860
All the complexity
is on display here,

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.750
and now it's your
job to figure out

00:11:24.750 --> 00:11:27.060
how you're going to assemble
all those pieces together

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:27.780
to make a castle.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.700
You've got no choice but to
go and ask someone to help.

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.700
And they say, yeah, get
1,000 of the gray bricks

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:34.922
and 1,000 of the brown
bricks, whatever else,

00:11:34.922 --> 00:11:36.130
and you're in there for ages.

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:39.180
And when you walk out, you have
this big bag of all this LEGO,

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.169
and you're still
not even sure how

00:11:41.169 --> 00:11:43.710
it's going to come together to
create the thing that you need

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:46.830
to give to your niece in spite
of spending ages and ages

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:48.252
in the store.

00:11:48.252 --> 00:11:50.400
So the outside-in
perspective would've

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.710
seen that a store full
of buckets of bricks

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:56.760
is too complex for folks looking
to buy a gift for their child.

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.880
As with APIs, to
create a great product,

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:04.740
it is important to bring
the outside-in perspective

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:06.412
to the development team.

00:12:06.412 --> 00:12:08.370
So one organization that's
been able to do this

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:11.340
with great result is
Walgreens, and Erin is here

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:13.565
today to tell us a little
bit about that journey.

00:12:13.565 --> 00:12:14.940
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG:
Thanks, Alicia.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.250
So as you're probably
aware, Walgreens

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:19.819
is a very large and very
diverse organization.

00:12:19.819 --> 00:12:21.360
And within the
company, we have a lot

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.140
of different businesses
operating independently

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.890
within the overall corporation.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:29.970
So my team, the core digital
products team, our mission

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.970
is to create and
scale and operate

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:35.080
the products and features
that aren't aligned

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.950
to anyone's specific business,
but those that support and add

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.360
value across all
of our businesses.

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.720
And one very important
component of my team

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:45.690
is our third party
API program, which

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.280
is what I am excited to
talk to you about today.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:52.770
So I've been in my current
role for just a few months now,

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:55.200
but I've been with Walgreens
for almost seven years now.

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:00.990
So I was there back in 2012 when
we launched our very first API,

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:04.260
and I remember it seemed
very novel at the time.

00:13:04.260 --> 00:13:06.050
So I was reading a
tech blog and there

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:09.240
was a headline that said,
"The apocalypse is upon us,

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:10.794
Walgreens has an API."

00:13:10.794 --> 00:13:12.210
So it might not
have always seemed

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:14.640
obvious from the
outside, but really

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:16.920
from inside the
organization, the conditions

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:19.920
were really right for us
to embark on this journey.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:23.070
And there are, I think, three
key things that really set us

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:24.580
up for success to do that.

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:27.000
So the first thing
is that we had

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:30.000
a really strong foundational
digital strategy

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.935
and mobile strategy
that we're building off

00:13:31.935 --> 00:13:34.140
of toward an API program.

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.200
Secondly, we had the right
mindset within the organization

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:40.020
to embrace disruption
and think about new

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:41.670
and better ways of doing things.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:46.170
And third, we were in a stage
of maturity within e-commerce

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:49.260
where we were really developing
and building out the product

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:51.180
management discipline
fundamentally.

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:53.130
So the timing was really great.

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:55.660
And let me just walk, in
a little bit more detail,

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:58.550
through each of
those with you here.

00:13:58.550 --> 00:14:02.090
So the first is our
API program strategy.

00:14:02.090 --> 00:14:06.340
So at the time, we had
some very, very innovative,

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:10.390
first-to-market-type digital
solutions that we had come up

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:13.540
with, like the ability
to scan your pill bottle

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:16.450
and automatically refill
your prescription that way,

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:19.150
and the ability to,
[CLEARING THROAT] excuse me,

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:21.970
print photos directly from
your camera roll directly

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:23.200
into the Walgreens store.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.780
And we came up with these
ideas and opportunities,

00:14:25.780 --> 00:14:30.310
really, by just re-imagining
those core central value

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:33.250
proposition elements that we
had in our retail locations

00:14:33.250 --> 00:14:35.260
and thought about how
they would manifest

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:37.420
as a great digital experience.

00:14:37.420 --> 00:14:39.940
And then beyond building
out those capabilities,

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:43.690
just due to the nature of the
features that we had built,

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:46.330
we really had to connect
them from the very beginning

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:47.910
into our physical location.

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:51.580
So we had a really rich and
robust Omnichannel experience

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:53.020
from very early on.

00:14:53.020 --> 00:14:56.460
And this was extremely powerful
for us, as you can imagine.

00:14:56.460 --> 00:14:59.770
And kind of beyond
just connecting

00:14:59.770 --> 00:15:02.140
our own digital
experiences, our leadership

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:04.300
in e-commerce at
the time realized

00:15:04.300 --> 00:15:07.720
that it could be even more
powerful if we could unlock

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:10.630
those core capabilities
to an even wider audience

00:15:10.630 --> 00:15:13.540
by connecting to this
broader developer ecosystem.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.960
So the second piece that I
think was really critical

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:24.210
for us was having a business
that was the right opportunity

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:26.860
to pursue this
type of innovation.

00:15:26.860 --> 00:15:30.750
And so we did have a
business-- photo, at the time,

00:15:30.750 --> 00:15:32.730
was going through a
massive transformation.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:35.010
If you're thinking back
to then, had very quickly

00:15:35.010 --> 00:15:39.390
gone from a state where people
were developing rolls of film

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:42.420
from cameras to doing
digital photography.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:44.910
And very quickly then,
even right through that,

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:48.390
into taking all of your photos
straight from your smartphone.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:51.300
And coupled with the
smartphone photography

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:53.850
was this development
of hundreds,

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:57.510
if not thousands, of different
photo taking and editing

00:15:57.510 --> 00:16:00.150
and collage apps
that were happening.

00:16:00.150 --> 00:16:04.230
And then the output of that
being so easy to just, with one

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.340
click, share to social media.

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:08.820
So that was your entire
experience from capture

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.310
through editing to
sharing, and it sort of

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:15.080
had elbowed out this need to
print a physical product, which

00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:18.900
had been the focal
point of taking photos,

00:16:18.900 --> 00:16:21.090
in the not too distant past.

00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:27.600
And so we were looking for
ways to solve that riddle.

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:31.860
And I give a ton of credit to
the head of our photo business

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:35.310
at that time-- is that he really
understood and appreciated

00:16:35.310 --> 00:16:37.620
that this change wasn't
necessarily a problem for us.

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:39.450
It was actually a
really big opportunity,

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:44.070
and he embraced that and was
really excited to see what

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:46.150
a program like this could do.

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:49.290
And honestly, right now I think
that almost every industry

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:51.280
is being disrupted
in this kind of way--

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:51.720
ALICIA PATERSON:
Of course, yeah.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:51.930
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG:
--so it would be

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.420
hard to even think of a business
that this kind of solution

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.840
couldn't be appropriate for.

00:16:57.840 --> 00:16:59.836
So the third piece
for us, again,

00:16:59.836 --> 00:17:01.710
was the fact that we
were really building out

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.260
this product management
discipline and way of thinking

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:06.150
within the organization.

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:08.220
And we kind of
looked at everything

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:11.940
as how do we manage this
thing in a product way?

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:15.430
And I've been in products
for a very, very long time,

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:18.420
and I'm accustomed to
identifying opportunities

00:17:18.420 --> 00:17:23.369
as the things that form the
intersection of the features

00:17:23.369 --> 00:17:25.109
that would specifically
drive value

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:30.510
for the business and the things
that the customer value is

00:17:30.510 --> 00:17:31.530
coming from.

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:34.320
And what I think is really
interesting about managing

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:38.850
API's products is that it layers
on this third dimension, which

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:42.690
is the things that drive
value for the developer.

00:17:42.690 --> 00:17:46.486
And in fact, it's really
different because the value--

00:17:46.486 --> 00:17:48.360
we're used to driving
value for the customer,

00:17:48.360 --> 00:17:50.370
but the value that you
drive for the customer

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:51.960
is actually indirect.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:54.540
And so you spend your
time just thinking

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:58.270
about what the possibilities
could be for that opportunity

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:00.870
and then making the best
possible product that

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:05.310
meets the needs of the developer
to unlock that and manage

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:06.750
that customer experience.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:07.450
ALICIA PATERSON:
Yeah, that's great.

00:18:07.450 --> 00:18:09.020
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: So
that's the outside-in

00:18:09.020 --> 00:18:10.420
that you were talking
about earlier.

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:11.795
ALICIA PATERSON:
Of course, yeah.

00:18:11.795 --> 00:18:14.655
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: So the
example that you see here, we

00:18:14.655 --> 00:18:16.530
thought about what our
problem statement was,

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:19.410
which is how do we reengage
these smartphone users

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.390
with photo printing?

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.000
And we looked at
our business value

00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:25.875
component, which is we really--

00:18:25.875 --> 00:18:28.020
we need to increase revenue
from photo services.

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:30.250
We need to get people
printing again,

00:18:30.250 --> 00:18:32.110
and what we thought
the customers wanted--

00:18:32.110 --> 00:18:34.604
which was a fun and
simple way to keep

00:18:34.604 --> 00:18:36.270
doing those things
that they were really

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:40.350
enjoying but to potentially
layer on a very easy way

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:43.380
to have a physical
component of output

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:47.250
from what they had
created that captures

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:49.940
not just what they took the
photo of in the first place,

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:53.370
but the experience of
actually creating and having

00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:56.650
this sort of social
environment in which they

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:58.320
were using their photos.

00:18:58.320 --> 00:19:04.020
And so then we really
thought about the fact

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:06.700
that we needed that
partnership with the developers

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.940
because that's where
the customers were.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:11.100
And we realized
that this could work

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:13.320
because we had a lot of
value that we could offer

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:15.810
to the developer community.

00:19:15.810 --> 00:19:19.410
And so we embarked on
our very first API.

00:19:19.410 --> 00:19:22.840
And as it turned out,
this formula that we had,

00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:24.900
which was the right
strategy, the right mindset,

00:19:24.900 --> 00:19:27.960
and the product management
approach, really worked for us.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:32.100
And because we had created this
great product with that outcome

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:34.190
in mind, it was easy to use.

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:36.300
It had a great
feature set, there

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:38.940
was an opportunity
for earning revenue

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:42.360
from the developers, which was
actually pretty rare back then.

00:19:42.360 --> 00:19:45.150
And the marketing opportunities
that came from a partnership

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:48.780
with the Walgreens brand,
we had tons of interest

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:49.590
in the program.

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:51.840
And in fact, just
in terms of scale,

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:54.600
we actually have had
over 200 integrations

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:58.705
just for our photo printing
API since we launched it.

00:19:58.705 --> 00:20:00.330
ALICIA PATERSON:
That's a great result.

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.790
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: And we've
seen a ton of benefits

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:06.510
from this API and the program
in general, specifically here.

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:09.660
There was a significant
amount of direct revenue

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:12.630
that started coming from this
channel, and still to this day,

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:16.500
is actually driving measurable
impact and influencing,

00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:19.320
bringing photo back to being a
growth business for us again.

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:21.150
ALICIA PATERSON:
Yeah, that's awesome.

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:24.180
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG:
And secondly, we

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.160
have found that the
developer community is

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:30.090
very good at identifying
new ways and opportunities

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:33.490
to attract customers
back into wanting

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:36.000
to print, which is great.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.220
And they're doing this
because we're giving them

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:41.160
these great tools and the
flexibility to problem

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:43.175
solve on their
own without having

00:20:43.175 --> 00:20:45.420
the ideas have to come from us.

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:48.750
And then finally, I think
that this kind of partnership

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:51.030
has really helped the
industry as a whole

00:20:51.030 --> 00:20:53.256
to become more innovative.

00:20:53.256 --> 00:20:55.380
And some of the innovation
that's come out of that,

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:57.600
we've actually been able
to repurpose and bring

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:02.010
into our own digital
assets as well.

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:06.630
So one more comment
on this, honestly.

00:21:06.630 --> 00:21:09.030
I'm telling the story
now as the perspective

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:11.880
of being leader of the team
that includes the API program.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:13.380
But back then, I was not that.

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:15.390
I wasn't a mobile
product person.

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:18.530
I wasn't an API product person.

00:21:18.530 --> 00:21:20.490
I was the photo product person.

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:22.320
And so it was really
difficult for me

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:26.040
to give up that
control of my customer,

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:31.050
that I had guarded so carefully,
to completely external parties

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:34.080
who were actually defining
parts of the customer journey.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:37.170
But at the same time, it was
actually really liberating

00:21:37.170 --> 00:21:40.410
because there were
things that I couldn't

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:42.270
do that maybe I would
have wanted to do

00:21:42.270 --> 00:21:44.370
and I couldn't because
of brand constraints

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:46.050
or because of
resource constraints

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:48.150
or because of
technology constraints.

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:49.775
And a lot of these
partners that we had

00:21:49.775 --> 00:21:51.240
didn't have the
same constraints,

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:53.130
and it was really
excited to see what they

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:55.260
could do with that freedom.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:57.760
And really, at the
end of the day,

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:00.600
regardless of where the
customer journey started,

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:03.510
at the end of it, they were
walking into a Walgreens store.

00:22:03.510 --> 00:22:05.580
And so they were getting
a really important part

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:08.160
of our customer journey
that was owned by us.

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.070
ALICIA PATERSON: Yeah,
that's wonderful.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:13.560
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: So over
the past five plus years,

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:17.580
we've continued to build out
our portfolio of API products,

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:20.370
and we've continued to
identify opportunities

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:23.940
to extend those core central
capabilities of Walgreens,

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:28.940
particularly in areas of
transition or disruption

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.950
in a way that's enticing
to the developer community.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:35.340
So today we have this whole
portfolio of API products

00:22:35.340 --> 00:22:36.780
that we manage.

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:38.910
For photo, we didn't
stop with prints.

00:22:38.910 --> 00:22:42.510
So almost immediately after
we launched the printing API,

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:45.060
our developer partners
started approaching us

00:22:45.060 --> 00:22:46.717
with ideas for other
types of products

00:22:46.717 --> 00:22:48.300
that they'd like to
be able to create,

00:22:48.300 --> 00:22:52.760
like greeting cards, posters,
canvases, photo books.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:57.210
And this was really
great because

00:22:57.210 --> 00:23:00.570
our organizational
expertise within Walgreens

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:04.860
was not necessarily creating
mobile photo product builders.

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.860
And we were really excited
because we had these products

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:09.060
that we could create.

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:11.268
We couldn't always get to
them, but we could actually

00:23:11.268 --> 00:23:13.050
let our partners be
the ones to focus

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:17.400
on things like building the best
mobile photo collage creator.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:19.080
And they were able
to actually launch

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:21.105
those products before
we could launch it

00:23:21.105 --> 00:23:23.460
in our own mobile app.

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:27.870
So after photo, we moved
on into the pharmacy space.

00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:30.910
Obviously this is big and
central to our business.

00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:36.180
So we initially went out
with a prescription refill

00:23:36.180 --> 00:23:38.209
and transfer API.

00:23:38.209 --> 00:23:39.750
And obviously anything
that has to do

00:23:39.750 --> 00:23:42.510
with pharmacy and Walgreens,
the scale and the reach

00:23:42.510 --> 00:23:46.560
is so great that it's a very
appealing partnership for any

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:47.760
of our potential partners.

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:50.360
And specifically,
our large health

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:53.400
care provider
partners are motivated

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:56.640
to try to drive patients
to take their medications

00:23:56.640 --> 00:23:58.330
in a timely fashion,
for example.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:00.630
So any way they
can reduce friction

00:24:00.630 --> 00:24:05.310
in the process of
getting the recommended

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:07.050
prescription into
a Walgreens store

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:09.480
to be filled without any
of those drop off points

00:24:09.480 --> 00:24:12.930
that it would require from
actually physically making

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:14.950
someone go to a
separate experience.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:18.960
And so a huge interest
in that program.

00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:21.190
Beyond that, other
aspects of health

00:24:21.190 --> 00:24:24.540
care as well, beyond
just prescriptions.

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:27.960
We went through a period
where we kicked off

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:31.080
a tremendous health care
innovation program, which

00:24:31.080 --> 00:24:33.300
has continued on to this day.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:37.170
One aspect of that was really
partnering, potentially,

00:24:37.170 --> 00:24:40.950
with fitness device
tracking companies.

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.800
And so looking for ways
to engage in health care

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.710
and wellness outside of
just the medications,

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:47.970
this was a great opportunity.

00:24:47.970 --> 00:24:51.390
So we actually launched what
we call our Balance Rewards

00:24:51.390 --> 00:24:55.740
for healthy choices API, which
means that a customer can

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:58.890
actually sync up and
integrate their fitness

00:24:58.890 --> 00:25:01.710
device with this API
and automatically

00:25:01.710 --> 00:25:05.250
be rewarded with our
loyalty program points which

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:08.222
they can then apply toward
rewards within the company--

00:25:08.222 --> 00:25:09.680
ALICIA PATERSON:
That's really cool

00:25:09.680 --> 00:25:11.555
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: --which
is really exciting.

00:25:11.555 --> 00:25:14.310
So moving beyond
that, most recently

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:18.240
we've been spending a lot
of time working on shopping

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:20.040
and couponing experiences.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:24.510
And so just kind of going
back to the mindset component,

00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:29.910
retail shopping, deal
seeking is shifting

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:32.460
a lot right now in terms of
the way customers approach it.

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:35.850
And they're definitely, in
most cases, not coming straight

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:38.950
to walgreens.com to do
all of that research.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:42.990
And so we need to find new
ways to get our experiences out

00:25:42.990 --> 00:25:44.880
there where customers
are searching

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.520
and shopping and deal-seeking
to bring them back

00:25:47.520 --> 00:25:49.090
into our network.

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:50.730
And so a couple of
things that we have,

00:25:50.730 --> 00:25:52.470
number one is there's
a Digital Offers

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:56.550
API, which enables us
to showcase personalized

00:25:56.550 --> 00:26:00.060
offers for customers which they
can then clip to their loyalty

00:26:00.060 --> 00:26:03.170
account and have that
coupon automatically

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:05.250
be applied at checkout.

00:26:05.250 --> 00:26:09.110
We also have an Add
to Cart API, so we're

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:12.380
able to actually transfer
a product directly

00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:16.700
from any product experience
into a walgreens.com

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:20.270
cart for fulfillment
by our business,

00:26:20.270 --> 00:26:22.730
so thinking a lot more there.

00:26:22.730 --> 00:26:25.290
We do have a few more really
exciting things coming,

00:26:25.290 --> 00:26:28.220
so definitely stay tuned.

00:26:28.220 --> 00:26:31.340
And I just wanted
to add, as we take

00:26:31.340 --> 00:26:34.910
a look at this whole portfolio
of APIs that we have here,

00:26:34.910 --> 00:26:37.460
is that we weren't just
coming to the table

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:40.910
and saying, hey, let's build
an API out of the blue, right?

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:44.300
We were actually doing this in
a way to respond to the things

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:46.850
that the company was focusing
on and thinking about,

00:26:46.850 --> 00:26:49.760
and again, especially
as these spaces

00:26:49.760 --> 00:26:51.950
were going through a
lot of transformation.

00:26:51.950 --> 00:26:55.070
But I think that
actually planning out

00:26:55.070 --> 00:26:59.690
how to productize a
service as an external API

00:26:59.690 --> 00:27:04.220
has helped us really get very
good at building our APIs to be

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:08.180
really flexible and scalable,
not just for external,

00:27:08.180 --> 00:27:10.760
but for internal use as well.

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:15.740
And what's really great
is that our API team, both

00:27:15.740 --> 00:27:17.240
on the product and
engineering side,

00:27:17.240 --> 00:27:21.680
has gotten really, really good
at encouraging our developer

00:27:21.680 --> 00:27:25.160
community within Walgreens
to think about this approach

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:27.620
and make things very
developer-friendly

00:27:27.620 --> 00:27:29.750
from the beginning, even
if we aren't originally

00:27:29.750 --> 00:27:33.150
intending it to surface it
outside of the organization.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:36.500
So just as an example,
we had a vendor

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:39.140
who's responsible for
replenishing water

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:42.080
coolers in our store
locations, and they

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:45.470
wanted to use our store
locating service that we had

00:27:45.470 --> 00:27:47.300
that we were using internally.

00:27:47.300 --> 00:27:49.790
And using Apigee,
we were actually

00:27:49.790 --> 00:27:52.760
able to open that up
to them very quickly

00:27:52.760 --> 00:27:55.290
and easily to
accomplish that goal.

00:27:55.290 --> 00:27:57.590
And so just one
final comment that I

00:27:57.590 --> 00:28:00.890
want to make with respect
to this whole portfolio

00:28:00.890 --> 00:28:06.530
is that on top of just building
the roadmap and the features

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:08.480
and building out
these capabilities,

00:28:08.480 --> 00:28:10.910
we're constantly
focusing on how we

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:13.940
can make it more
developer-friendly and how,

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:15.770
as you mentioned
before, how important it

00:28:15.770 --> 00:28:19.910
is to make that onboarding
process as smooth as possible.

00:28:19.910 --> 00:28:21.830
Because we know we
have a lot of value

00:28:21.830 --> 00:28:24.770
here that we can offer
to developer partners,

00:28:24.770 --> 00:28:27.402
and we just don't want there
to be any obstacles to them

00:28:27.402 --> 00:28:28.235
for getting onboard.

00:28:31.540 --> 00:28:34.390
So just wanted to kind
of talk a little bit

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:37.030
about what I think has helped
us be successful managing

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:38.830
APIs as products.

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:40.720
And I know that
every organization

00:28:40.720 --> 00:28:43.120
is a little different-- the
resources, the structure

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:44.270
is a little bit different.

00:28:44.270 --> 00:28:46.600
But here's some of
the things that I

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:48.580
thought would be
applicable more broadly

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:52.130
and kind of what I think
makes our API product roadmap

00:28:52.130 --> 00:28:53.860
a success within Walgreens.

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:57.640
So the first piece is that
we have a product roadmap.

00:28:57.640 --> 00:29:01.450
So again, we manage it just
like any other product.

00:29:01.450 --> 00:29:03.490
We have a backlog,
we have a roadmap.

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:06.880
We integrate with the digital
roadmap planning process

00:29:06.880 --> 00:29:11.230
that we have across all of
our digital product teams.

00:29:11.230 --> 00:29:13.870
And we participate
in an event that we

00:29:13.870 --> 00:29:17.740
do quarterly, which is basically
a quarterly digital product

00:29:17.740 --> 00:29:20.200
roadmap review,
and that includes

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:22.840
not just the digital
product team, but all

00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:25.180
of our different core
business stakeholders

00:29:25.180 --> 00:29:26.890
across the different
businesses--

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:29.290
IT partners, marketing partners.

00:29:29.290 --> 00:29:31.120
And I think that
visibility into what we're

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:35.440
doing with our API program has
driven really great engagement.

00:29:35.440 --> 00:29:39.070
Secondly, we have a really
great developer evangelist

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:42.310
who is actually a member
of our product team,

00:29:42.310 --> 00:29:45.400
so he's an engineer and also
a product thinking person.

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:48.010
And I think that partnership
has been really, really critical

00:29:48.010 --> 00:29:55.300
for us to be able to not
only think about the APIs

00:29:55.300 --> 00:29:58.990
in a product way, but also for
him to be aware of what we're

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:01.510
bringing out into the market.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:05.260
And beyond that, we have
really, really great tools

00:30:05.260 --> 00:30:07.360
that we use that
help us effectively

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:09.610
manage the program so we
can spend as much time

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:11.590
as possible thinking
strategically and less

00:30:11.590 --> 00:30:14.120
about the administrative
part of the job.

00:30:14.120 --> 00:30:16.240
So we have our
Developer Portal, which

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:18.400
is the entry point for
the developer community

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:24.550
to come into our API program
and to interface with us.

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:27.130
Of course we have
Apigee, which allows

00:30:27.130 --> 00:30:31.540
us to be a really good partner
to our developer community.

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:34.780
We have a pretty small team,
and we have a lot of partners.

00:30:34.780 --> 00:30:36.730
We have over 300
partners right now.

00:30:36.730 --> 00:30:40.870
And so Apigee does a
great job of making

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:42.580
that really seamless
and efficient

00:30:42.580 --> 00:30:47.620
for us to managing across
that large portfolio,

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:49.510
and furthermore,
for us to get really

00:30:49.510 --> 00:30:53.335
easy access into the data and
analytics around the program.

00:30:55.900 --> 00:30:58.110
We also have a really
great central communication

00:30:58.110 --> 00:30:59.160
tool that we use.

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.500
We use Basecamp,
so it helps anyone

00:31:01.500 --> 00:31:06.720
who is interested in following
these things from set

00:31:06.720 --> 00:31:08.447
up and launch of
these capabilities

00:31:08.447 --> 00:31:10.280
as it goes through
different types of tasks,

00:31:10.280 --> 00:31:13.450
some more technical or
less technical than others.

00:31:13.450 --> 00:31:15.090
It's all in one
place, so it makes

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:18.879
it really seamless to track that
through from beginning to end.

00:31:18.879 --> 00:31:20.670
And then finally, from
a tools perspective,

00:31:20.670 --> 00:31:24.000
we recently launched more
of an Informational Portal

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:27.672
that's less technical
than our Developer Portal.

00:31:27.672 --> 00:31:29.130
And this is something
that we think

00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:31.320
is going to be helpful
both internally

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:34.590
and for our developer community
to be able to talk in more

00:31:34.590 --> 00:31:38.850
value centric language about
what this API program really is

00:31:38.850 --> 00:31:40.309
and the benefits
that it can drive.

00:31:40.309 --> 00:31:42.225
ALICIA PATERSON: Yes,
connecting more directly

00:31:42.225 --> 00:31:43.030
with actual value.

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:44.238
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Absolutely.

00:31:44.238 --> 00:31:46.830
And so last but
definitely not least,

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:51.690
we put a big focus on, as
you do with any product,

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:55.470
defining what success
looks like for that product

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:58.500
and being able to report
out the performance

00:31:58.500 --> 00:32:00.030
against the key
metrics that we've

00:32:00.030 --> 00:32:02.160
defined from the beginning.

00:32:02.160 --> 00:32:03.870
And as interest
in the program has

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:07.770
grown beyond digital
product management,

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:11.070
we've started actually putting
together more business centric

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:14.940
dashboards, and sometimes in
partnership with our marketing

00:32:14.940 --> 00:32:17.715
teams as well to really
kind of polish this

00:32:17.715 --> 00:32:20.520
and to be able to very clearly
articulate the business

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:23.010
value and the success
that these programs have

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:25.050
and that they can have.

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:27.450
So hopefully that
helps shed some light

00:32:27.450 --> 00:32:31.300
on how we manage our API
products within Walgreens.

00:32:31.300 --> 00:32:36.000
And I'm excited to answer a few
questions about the program,

00:32:36.000 --> 00:32:38.100
so thank you for having me.

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:39.749
And definitely I
would suggest also,

00:32:39.749 --> 00:32:41.790
if I don't have time to
answer all the questions,

00:32:41.790 --> 00:32:44.910
you can always reach
out to our team

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:48.025
through our Developer Portal
at developer.walgreens.com.

00:32:48.025 --> 00:32:49.400
ALICIA PATERSON:
Thank you, Erin.

00:32:49.400 --> 00:32:51.441
It's always great to hear
your story because it's

00:32:51.441 --> 00:32:53.640
great to hear an
organization like Walgreens

00:32:53.640 --> 00:32:56.250
create this platform
and then build

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:59.360
an ecosystem of participants.

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:01.930
They're actually adding
value to your business,

00:33:01.930 --> 00:33:05.205
but also incentivizing them
to continue to contribute.

00:33:05.205 --> 00:33:06.330
So it's such a great story.

00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:08.210
I love sharing it with people.

00:33:08.210 --> 00:33:11.146
So I did mention before,
we have some resources

00:33:11.146 --> 00:33:12.270
I wanted to share with you.

00:33:12.270 --> 00:33:16.260
So we have "The Complete
Guide to API Products eBook.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:17.880
The link is available
on the screen

00:33:17.880 --> 00:33:20.010
there, so I encourage
you to go and look.

00:33:20.010 --> 00:33:24.720
There's a much deeper dive
there into how to build and run

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:28.770
API programs as API products.

00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:30.602
And there's a microsite
that's around that

00:33:30.602 --> 00:33:33.060
as well that's sort of dedicated
to additional information.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:35.760
There's some customer
stories and blogs there.

00:33:35.760 --> 00:33:38.670
And of course,
developer.walgreens.com

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:42.430
to get to Walgreens's
Developer Portal.

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:44.810
So now I'd ask you to please
stay tuned for the live

00:33:44.810 --> 00:33:47.250
Q&amp;A. We'll be back for that
in just less than a minute.

00:35:01.240 --> 00:35:03.770
So thank you for some of the
questions I see rolling in.

00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:05.830
So I'm showing some of
those on the screen,

00:35:05.830 --> 00:35:08.400
and I'm going to ask questions,
and Erin will probably

00:35:08.400 --> 00:35:10.110
respond for most of them.

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:12.960
So first question, Erin, how
do you manage the bureaucracy

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:16.020
within a large
organization like Walgreens

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:17.760
when looking to build
new API products

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:19.100
and expose additional services?

00:35:19.100 --> 00:35:22.570
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Yeah, that is
definitely a big factor for us.

00:35:22.570 --> 00:35:25.470
And to me, the key is really
always about relationships.

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:27.900
So I think that's one thing
that product managers are

00:35:27.900 --> 00:35:31.440
trained to do really well
is to build relationships

00:35:31.440 --> 00:35:33.450
across different parts
of the organization

00:35:33.450 --> 00:35:36.160
and to actually speak a
lot of different languages,

00:35:36.160 --> 00:35:37.260
so to speak.

00:35:37.260 --> 00:35:40.560
And it's really important
to be able to communicate

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:44.610
not just what we're doing but
actually why we're doing it

00:35:44.610 --> 00:35:47.100
and really lead with
the value proposition

00:35:47.100 --> 00:35:50.640
and what the benefit is to
the organization by doing

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:53.160
and what we're unlocking
with this given

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:54.960
service or capability.

00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:58.530
And beyond that, I think
the team has really

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:02.670
done a great job of building
subject matter expertise

00:36:02.670 --> 00:36:03.720
and credibility.

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:06.450
And we make sure that we
have those trained experts

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:09.330
present throughout the
process, whether it's

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:12.510
trying to get a security
approval or some other part

00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:13.410
of the process.

00:36:13.410 --> 00:36:18.150
And building that trust with
all of our partners really

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:21.330
helps us move the path
forward through some

00:36:21.330 --> 00:36:23.340
of that bureaucracy
that can be in place.

00:36:23.340 --> 00:36:24.090
ALICIA PATERSON: And
of course and they

00:36:24.090 --> 00:36:26.280
say that nothing breeds
success like success,

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:28.830
and you've had such
a successful story.

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:31.050
But to your point
earlier, being able to tie

00:36:31.050 --> 00:36:33.900
what you have done back
to real demonstrated value

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:36.870
for the business, that gives
you a platform internally to go

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:38.540
and talk about your success.

00:36:38.540 --> 00:36:40.170
All right, next question.

00:36:40.170 --> 00:36:42.750
Were you able to
estimate or quantify

00:36:42.750 --> 00:36:45.780
the assumed benefits of
the program of enabling out

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:48.660
to external developers prior
to going down this path?

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:50.550
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG:
Yeah, so it can

00:36:50.550 --> 00:36:55.080
be really hard to do that
because you don't necessarily

00:36:55.080 --> 00:36:56.622
control all aspects
of the market.

00:36:56.622 --> 00:36:58.080
You can't have a
marketing campaign

00:36:58.080 --> 00:37:00.420
that you plan out, necessarily.

00:37:00.420 --> 00:37:02.160
It's much more
organic than that.

00:37:02.160 --> 00:37:03.750
And so what you
really have to do

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:07.320
is focus on the potential value.

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:10.410
And you can have,
after you define

00:37:10.410 --> 00:37:13.650
what success would
look like for this API,

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:15.900
you can have an
addressable market

00:37:15.900 --> 00:37:18.510
and you can have a target
customer, which actually might

00:37:18.510 --> 00:37:20.340
be a target developer partner.

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:23.070
So you can kind of put that
framework loosely in place.

00:37:23.070 --> 00:37:26.867
But then once you've done that
from a potential standpoint,

00:37:26.867 --> 00:37:28.950
you really just have to
focus on building the best

00:37:28.950 --> 00:37:32.760
product that's most enticing
and engaging for the developer

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:34.980
and putting that effort into
it so that you're really

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:37.650
realizing that potential
value that you've established.

00:37:37.650 --> 00:37:39.274
ALICIA PATERSON: And
of course, and you

00:37:39.274 --> 00:37:40.810
can't plan for innovation.

00:37:40.810 --> 00:37:42.310
And to your point
earlier, there's

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:44.700
probably far more innovators
outside the organization

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:46.890
than internally, so
you found some people

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:49.860
doing interesting things
you weren't anticipating.

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:53.010
But of course, being
[INAUDIBLE] set of customers,

00:37:53.010 --> 00:37:56.340
being able to try-- get very,
very close to the end user,

00:37:56.340 --> 00:37:58.581
to the business
need to developers,

00:37:58.581 --> 00:38:01.080
and then be able to interact
quickly on how you support them

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:02.124
with API products.

00:38:02.124 --> 00:38:03.790
I think that ability
to iterate quickly,

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:05.720
you'll find where the
value is, and then

00:38:05.720 --> 00:38:08.105
you can start building
on top of that.

00:38:08.105 --> 00:38:08.980
So the next question.

00:38:08.980 --> 00:38:10.860
Erin, with the
benefit of experience,

00:38:10.860 --> 00:38:14.010
if you go back to the start
of the Walgreens API program,

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:16.107
is there anything that
you would do differently?

00:38:16.107 --> 00:38:17.940
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Yeah,
so honestly, I mean,

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:19.320
I'd say not a lot.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:21.090
And I think you saw
in my story that we

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:23.130
got really lucky,
that we actually

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:24.755
had a lot of the
right pieces in place.

00:38:24.755 --> 00:38:26.588
And I think we did a
lot of the right things

00:38:26.588 --> 00:38:27.480
from the beginning.

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:31.260
And I understand
that not everyone

00:38:31.260 --> 00:38:33.801
might have that good
fortune, to have

00:38:33.801 --> 00:38:35.050
all the right things in place.

00:38:35.050 --> 00:38:36.930
So I would just
suggest for others,

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:39.870
while I wouldn't
necessarily change,

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:43.440
for another company to try
to replicate as many of those

00:38:43.440 --> 00:38:45.480
conditions that I
talked about as you can,

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:48.120
at least to the extent
that you can control it.

00:38:48.120 --> 00:38:53.730
So that being said, I
do think that if there

00:38:53.730 --> 00:38:55.980
was one thing that I could
do a little bit differently

00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:57.750
was maybe engaging
a little bit earlier

00:38:57.750 --> 00:39:00.440
with our digital marketing team
about our marketing strategy.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:03.780
I think there's some really
unique things here about how

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:07.740
you market API programs that I
think we've been still working

00:39:07.740 --> 00:39:10.646
through, which is
you really want

00:39:10.646 --> 00:39:11.770
the benefit of the program.

00:39:11.770 --> 00:39:15.510
You want the appetite and the
engagement for the program,

00:39:15.510 --> 00:39:18.600
but the brand that's driving
it isn't your own brand.

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:21.040
And so the considerations
are a little bit different.

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:22.410
And again, I think
we're getting there,

00:39:22.410 --> 00:39:23.993
but I think if we
had started earlier,

00:39:23.993 --> 00:39:26.880
we may have perfected that dance
a little bit better by now.

00:39:26.880 --> 00:39:28.380
ALICIA PATERSON:
It certainly sounds

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:31.470
like you're fortunate
to have great vision

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:34.140
for the organization in
terms of digital goals,

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:36.030
but then also, strong
leadership, right?

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:39.355
Both in your executive level and
your director level that were

00:39:39.355 --> 00:39:42.600
able to execute on the plans.

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:45.930
So Erin, how do you
communicate the value of APIs

00:39:45.930 --> 00:39:48.390
to business leadership and
to product stakeholders?

00:39:48.390 --> 00:39:50.250
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Yeah,
so I've definitely

00:39:50.250 --> 00:39:52.980
talked about this throughout the
presentation and a little bit

00:39:52.980 --> 00:39:55.530
earlier, but I think the
most important part of this

00:39:55.530 --> 00:39:57.310
is just speaking
the right language.

00:39:57.310 --> 00:40:00.410
So if you're talking
to a marketing person

00:40:00.410 --> 00:40:03.830
and you're talking about
things in terms of customer

00:40:03.830 --> 00:40:06.050
acquisition, and you
bring to the table

00:40:06.050 --> 00:40:09.260
an actual example with
data that shows how you've

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:12.500
been able to bring customers
into becoming customers

00:40:12.500 --> 00:40:15.200
that have, through
the API program,

00:40:15.200 --> 00:40:17.920
come in and driven broader
value for the company.

00:40:17.920 --> 00:40:21.530
And if you're talking to
a P&amp;L owner or a merchant,

00:40:21.530 --> 00:40:25.490
you're talking about things in
terms of EBIT and basket size

00:40:25.490 --> 00:40:28.400
and how you're driving
value for them.

00:40:28.400 --> 00:40:31.440
And when you're talking to
someone on the product team,

00:40:31.440 --> 00:40:34.580
you're talking about things
like rapid test and learn

00:40:34.580 --> 00:40:39.230
and outside innovation, and how
these developer partnerships

00:40:39.230 --> 00:40:45.470
have made our own products
more effective and better

00:40:45.470 --> 00:40:47.496
meet the needs of our customers.

00:40:47.496 --> 00:40:48.620
ALICIA PATERSON: OK, great.

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:50.210
I think we did touch
on measurements,

00:40:50.210 --> 00:40:51.560
but I'll ask this question
because I think this

00:40:51.560 --> 00:40:53.210
is from a very practical level.

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:55.451
What do we measure
in your API program?

00:40:55.451 --> 00:40:57.950
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Yeah, we have
some kind of standard things

00:40:57.950 --> 00:41:01.010
that we typically measure,
like the number of partners

00:41:01.010 --> 00:41:04.430
that we have for a given API
or the number of transactions

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:07.530
that are standard
across all of them.

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:09.620
And then we have
some unique things

00:41:09.620 --> 00:41:13.130
for each that are
applicable to that API,

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:17.080
like the number of rewards
points that have been awarded,

00:41:17.080 --> 00:41:19.670
the number of coupons
that have been redeemed,

00:41:19.670 --> 00:41:22.347
or the number of prescriptions
that have been refilled.

00:41:22.347 --> 00:41:23.930
ALICIA PATERSON:
Right, which is great

00:41:23.930 --> 00:41:25.554
because then you're
actually tying the,

00:41:25.554 --> 00:41:27.740
I guess, the transactions
on the API level

00:41:27.740 --> 00:41:31.039
back to real
business activities.

00:41:31.039 --> 00:41:33.080
And so the question was
how do you tie those back

00:41:33.080 --> 00:41:34.260
to business benefits?

00:41:34.260 --> 00:41:36.290
How do you measure
developer satisfaction?

00:41:36.290 --> 00:41:38.600
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Yes, so we
have a couple ways that we

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:40.500
measure developer satisfaction.

00:41:40.500 --> 00:41:45.110
So we actually have a standard
developer satisfaction survey

00:41:45.110 --> 00:41:48.530
that is just like a customer
satisfaction survey.

00:41:48.530 --> 00:41:51.590
And that goes out once a year,
and we evaluate the results

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:54.290
from that to help
build a better program.

00:41:54.290 --> 00:41:56.210
But beyond that,
I think, again, we

00:41:56.210 --> 00:41:58.400
have this really strong
developer evangelist,

00:41:58.400 --> 00:42:02.570
and he's out there every day
having meetings with some

00:42:02.570 --> 00:42:04.190
of our key developers.

00:42:04.190 --> 00:42:09.631
And I think we have a really
good sense and our finger

00:42:09.631 --> 00:42:11.630
on the pulse of what our
developers want because

00:42:11.630 --> 00:42:13.010
of those frequent interactions.

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:15.590
And almost any significant
developer, partner,

00:42:15.590 --> 00:42:19.280
in our ecosystem, we're
constantly asking them

00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:20.447
what we can be doing better.

00:42:20.447 --> 00:42:21.821
ALICIA PATERSON:
You know, that's

00:42:21.821 --> 00:42:24.860
such an important function
I don't see organizations

00:42:24.860 --> 00:42:25.679
consider enough.

00:42:25.679 --> 00:42:27.720
They sort of leave it as
an after thought almost,

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:30.040
like who's going to
wake up every day

00:42:30.040 --> 00:42:33.050
and think about how do I
make it easier for developers

00:42:33.050 --> 00:42:34.800
to engage with us [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:34.800 --> 00:42:37.425
So it's great that you
have that person in place.

00:42:37.425 --> 00:42:39.600
So Erin, how do you
stay close to developers

00:42:39.600 --> 00:42:42.200
to ensure an
outside-in perspective

00:42:42.200 --> 00:42:44.150
and ensure that you're
API product design is

00:42:44.150 --> 00:42:45.359
a strong fit for their needs?

00:42:45.359 --> 00:42:47.524
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Yeah, so
I think I mentioned this.

00:42:47.524 --> 00:42:49.520
I mean, we just have
such a close partnership

00:42:49.520 --> 00:42:52.110
on an ongoing basis
with our developers.

00:42:52.110 --> 00:42:55.580
And I think not just
listening to what

00:42:55.580 --> 00:42:58.460
they're asking for from us
but sort of sharing back

00:42:58.460 --> 00:43:00.350
to them the things
that we're thinking of.

00:43:00.350 --> 00:43:02.070
And it's really a dialogue.

00:43:02.070 --> 00:43:06.890
And so oftentimes we bring
certain partners in very, very

00:43:06.890 --> 00:43:09.000
early into the process--

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:12.590
and we've had launch partners
come in even when an API is

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:13.310
in beta--

00:43:13.310 --> 00:43:15.950
and I think we
just, together, we

00:43:15.950 --> 00:43:17.820
learn so much from each other.

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:21.280
And I think that helps us
understand the developer needs

00:43:21.280 --> 00:43:24.050
before we get too
far down the pipeline

00:43:24.050 --> 00:43:26.270
and that it is
meeting their needs

00:43:26.270 --> 00:43:28.220
and then also meeting our needs.

00:43:28.220 --> 00:43:30.470
ALICIA PATERSON: And do
you incentivize developers?

00:43:30.470 --> 00:43:31.678
ERIN NEUS-CHEONG: Yes, we do.

00:43:31.678 --> 00:43:34.380
So we have a few
ways that we do that.

00:43:34.380 --> 00:43:36.050
I think most
notably, we actually,

00:43:36.050 --> 00:43:38.790
for a couple of our APIs,
we have a revenue share.

00:43:38.790 --> 00:43:42.210
So there is an actual
monetary compensation.

00:43:42.210 --> 00:43:45.080
And I think there's other
ways of incentivizing.

00:43:45.080 --> 00:43:47.520
Again, the Walgreens
brand is very valuable,

00:43:47.520 --> 00:43:51.050
so being able to talk about a
partnership with the Walgreens

00:43:51.050 --> 00:43:53.730
brand is something
that is an incentive.

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:56.360
And then finally, those sort of
indirect incentives where we're

00:43:56.360 --> 00:43:59.390
enabling a business
metric that that partner

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:02.637
has through the services that
they can get through our APIs.

00:44:02.637 --> 00:44:03.970
ALICIA PATERSON: That's awesome.

00:44:03.970 --> 00:44:06.011
So actually, I think that's
probably all the time

00:44:06.011 --> 00:44:08.370
we have for questions.

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:12.480
So we will make sure to answer
our questions separately

00:44:12.480 --> 00:44:14.266
online, so you'll
see responses there.

00:44:14.266 --> 00:44:15.682
And we've got
Googlers, as I said,

00:44:15.682 --> 00:44:17.820
that are also
answering questions.

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:20.570
So I think that wraps us up.

00:44:20.570 --> 00:44:22.050
I'd like to thank
you once again,

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:25.010
everyone, for joining
into our webinar today.

00:44:25.010 --> 00:44:29.060
And thank you very much to Erin
for joining us and presenting

00:44:29.060 --> 00:44:32.090
your really amazing
Walgreens API product story.

00:44:32.090 --> 00:44:35.870
And a reminder that we
have another webcast again

00:44:35.870 --> 00:44:38.870
next Tuesday, and we hope
you can join us again then.

00:44:38.870 --> 00:44:40.550
Thank you.

00:44:40.550 --> 00:44:44.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

